{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788303984,"nonce":"kVPyQfMMS0ykizqVuu5UZK3K1D0=","title":"Nothing wakes a sleeping agent but a casualty: every letter and alert halt is opt-in, so a day-long advance sleeps through raids, traders and inspirations","message":"Found by the orchestrator on 2026-09-01 while ruling `1113019`, and **ruled by Evan in the same conversation**. This is the other half of that issue: once the default bound on an `until` advance is a full in-game day, the halt set becomes the thing that decides whether a day is safe to sleep through.\n\n## The measurement\n\n`Source/AutoRimmer/TimeDriver.cs`. Four halts fire **unconditionally**:\n\n| halt | trigger |\n|---|---|\n| `casualty` | own-faction `downed`/`death`, on the TRANSITION (`MakeDowned`/`SetDead` hooks), so it cannot re-fire for a pawn already down |\n| `dialog` | a force-pausing window — deliberately unconditional, since `OpenAutomaticLetters` is dead while one is up |\n| `red_error` | a red error, owned by `NoticeRedError` upstream of the journal's dedupe cap |\n| `thermal` | the governor stepping down — CPU protection, not a game event |\n\n**Everything else is OPT-IN.** `Halt(\"letter\", …)`, `Halt(\"threat\", …)`, `Halt(\"alert\", …)` and `Halt(\"event\", …)` all sit inside `switch (until)`, so they fire only if the caller pre-asked with `until:{letter}` / `{threat}` / `{alert}` / `{event}`.\n\nSo an agent that sleeps a day with `advance {ticks:60000}` is **not** woken by: a raid landing, any letter at all (trader, quest, disease, wanderer, inspiration), any alert firing (`Alert_LowFood`, no doctor, breakdown, mood collapse), fire, starvation, hypothermia, or a prisoner escaping. A raid arriving at hour 2 is discovered at hour 24, after the colony fought it alone — unless the agent guessed in advance that today was the day.\n\nThe design was coherent when advances were short and hand-driven. Moving the default bound to a day is what changes the calculus.\n\n## Evan's ruling, and it is not a severity filter\n\nI proposed a severity cut: wake on `ThreatBig`/`ThreatSmall`/`Death`/`NegativeEvent` letters and Critical/High alerts, on the grounds of noise. **Evan rejected the framing:**\n\n\u003e \"anything neutral or positive should wake you, maybe you want to act on an inspiration, things like that. that's how you get propelled into actually playing the game and having fun.\"\n\nThe rule is **\"is there something I might act on\"**, not \"is this bad\". An inspiration expires if you sleep through it. A trader leaves. A wanderer at the door is a roster decision. **A run that only ever wakes for disasters is one that survives ten days without ever playing.**\n\nThat simplifies the letter half to nothing: **halt on EVERY letter, no filter.** RimWorld only sends a letter when it thinks the player should look — the game already did the filtering, and re-filtering it second-guesses the one system that is good at this. It also removes an allow-list, which is a second source of truth this project has been burned by (see the `Build:` tally essay in the workspace CLAUDE.md, and `7382bdd`'s rejected arg whitelist).\n\nNoise is not the problem it sounds like. Measured on a bench being actively wrecked by an acceptance suite, 13,667 ticks — about half a day — produced **53 journal events total**, of which **3 letters** and **6 `alert_on`**.\n\n## Alerts are the one case that differs, and Evan ruled that too\n\nA letter is an event that happens once. An alert is a STANDING CONDITION. `alert_on` is a transition so a chronic alert wakes you once per on-cycle rather than continuously — but a condition you have deliberately decided not to fix will flicker off and on and wake you each time for a decision you already made.\n\n\u003e \"the agent should have the ability to blacklist what alerts wake them up, while they're playing.\"\n\n**A runtime blacklist, set by the agent mid-run.** Not static config. This is session 13's `threat-pardon` ruling applied again: **the decision must be a recorded ACT, not a silent exemption.**\n\n## Scope\n\n**1. Make the letter halt unconditional.** Move `Halt(\"letter\", …)` out of `switch (until)`. Every letter halts. `until:{letter}` and `until:{threat}` must keep working as explicit waits — they are a different question (\"wait FOR this\") and the two must not collide.\n\n**2. Make the alert halt unconditional**, on the `alert_on` transition, subject to the blacklist below.\n\n**3. A runtime alert blacklist, as a recorded act.** A verb that mutes and unmutes a named alert for waking purposes, taking a REQUIRED reason and journalling it, on the `threat-pardon` precedent. Design and justify: the verb name, whether a mute is permanent or expires, and what happens to an alert muted while it is already on.\n\n**The mute list MUST be visible in the digest.** A standing decision the agent cannot see is the `[[seek-off-is-a-decision-to-flee]]` failure exactly — `b1b3060` shipped `digest.posture` for precisely this reason. An agent that muted `Alert_LowFood` on day 2 and forgot must be able to see it on day 8.\n\n**4. The escape.** A per-call way to ride past, matching `722c951`'s `unread_ok` / `through_casualties` — required non-empty reason, journalled. Decide whether it is a third argument or an extension of `through_casualties`, and say why.\n\n**5. Interaction with `1113019`.** That issue defaults an unbounded `until` advance to 60,000 ticks. These halts are what make that default safe. Note in DESIGN that the two were ruled together and depend on each other.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- An advance spanning a letter of ANY def — including `NeutralEvent` and `PositiveEvent` — halts, and the halt names the letter.\n- An advance spanning an `alert_on` halts, and the halt names the alert and its priority.\n- `until:{letter}` and `until:{threat}` still work as explicit waits and are distinguishable from an unconditional halt.\n- A blacklisted alert does NOT halt, the blacklisting is journalled as an act with its reason, and it is visible in `digest`.\n- Un-blacklisting restores the halt; proven both ways.\n- The escape rides past a letter halt and is journalled.\n- A day-long advance on a quiet colony still completes without halting — prove the halts do not fire spuriously.\n- `accept/4.2-play-loop.py` can tell a letter halt from an alert halt from a casualty halt from a bound completion.","files":null}]}